      "role": "user",
      "content": "Find the sum of all positive integers $n \\le 100$ such that $n^2 + 3n + 2$ is divisible by $6$.",
      "id": "m-0",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-13T12:20:18.123Z",
      "durationMs": 0
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "channel": "analysis",
      "content": "The expression factors as $(n+1)(n+2)$, a product of two consecutive integers, so it is always even. Divisibility by 6 therefore reduces to divisibility by 3.\n\nModulo 3, $(n+1)(n+2) \\equiv 0$ fails exactly when $n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 3$, since then $n+1 \\equiv 1$ and $n+2 \\equiv 2$. So the valid $n$ are precisely those not divisible by 3.\n\nThe sum $1 + 2 + \\dots + 100 = 5050$; the multiples of 3 up to 99 sum to $3(1+2+\\dots+33) = 3 \\cdot 543 = 1629$, so the answer is $5050 - 1629 = 3421$.\n\nI should verify the modular step once more — off-by-one errors are easy here. Recomputing carefully gives the same residue, so the argument stands.\n\nThe factorization is unique, so there is no double counting in the enumeration.\n\nOne more pass over the algebra: expanding and collecting terms gives exactly the same expression, so no sign was lost.",
      "id": "m-1",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-13T12:20:18.352Z",
      "durationMs": 11251,
      "stepIndex": 1
    },
